# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
dist/
build/
.hypothesis/
.pytest_cache/

# Secrets & local data (NEVER commit)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
server/data/*.db
server/data/*.db-*
server/data/*.crt
server/data/*.csr

# `omem-server` writes ./omem-data in whatever directory it was started from,
# and QUICKSTART.md tells people to just run it — so for anyone who tries the
# quickstart inside a clone, that is right here. The file holds their memories,
# hashed API keys and org rows, and it arrived untracked and unmentioned, one
# `git add .` from being published. Ignored wherever it lands, not just at the
# root, because a contributor may run the server from server/ or anywhere else.
omem-data/
*.key
*.pem

# Node / web
node_modules/
.next/
out/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# Generated by sdk/python/hatch_build.py at build time (a copy of server/).
# Never commit it: server/ is the single source.
sdk/python/omem/_server/

# Built dashboard. web/out is the Next static export; omem/_dashboard is the
# copy hatch_build.py makes into the wheel. Both are generated — web/ is the
# single source, exactly as server/ is for the bundled server.
web/out/
sdk/python/omem/_dashboard/

# A build artifact: it changes on every typecheck and belongs to nobody.
web/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# Written by `omem-verify --record`. It is a digest of YOUR data at a moment
# in time, and anchoring it next to the database proves nothing against
# someone who can edit both. Keep the copy that matters somewhere else.
.omem-state.json

# Private business material: strategy, prospect lists, outbound drafts and
# unsent letters. It lives beside the code because it is written alongside it,
# and it must never travel with it. This entry exists because a subdirectory
# of it once reached a public pull request, and "remember not to add it" is not
# a control.
distribution/
# Third-party survey responses, fetched by the benchmark at run time and
# never redistributed here. The pattern rather than the filenames, because the
# next dataset added is otherwise committed by default -- which has now
# happened twice, at 151 MB, and both times the push is what caught it rather
# than the rule that was supposed to.
benchmarks/external/_*
adapters/langgraph/testimony_validate.py
